STEP N3 -- what the enable does to the saved configuration, and whether disabling it  done
           brings the lost lines back
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Three phases, and they are not the same.

  Phase 1, at enable time and before any restart. The running configuration changed in
  exactly two places and nowhere else:
    -service telnet
    -no service telnet ipv6
    +no service telnet
    +crypto secure-mode
  The v2c community, the SNMPv3 user, `username ckp7 privilege 7`, the NTP MD5 key and the
  whole `radius-server local` block were all still present. The enforcement matched that.
  Telnet on TCP/23 was already refused (`[Errno 111] Connection refused`) while SNMPv2c
  still returned a value and the local RADIUS server still returned Access-Accept. The CLI
  parser was completely unrestricted: `service telnet ?`, `username test7 privilege ?`
  (`<0-15>`) and `snmp-server user ... auth ?` (md5 present) were byte-identical to normal
  mode.

  Phase 2, at the restart. The device ANNOUNCED every line it refused. It did not drop
  them in silence. On the console during boot:
    WARNING: Failed to execute the following commands:
    username ckp7 privilege 7 password 8 $5$o8/iulS8Cg5389J0$UEXNLIlOLKEAr9Bx4ZhnAfgMBVTie4G1O9enTKrDMcA
    snmp-server community cktest
    snmp-server user ckv3 ckgrp encrypted auth sha 0x05de9451cf93eb8a79948bb495e765630266332868 priv aes 0x059c399342e8393e909b5b05bc9f55ed2c81fe7247
    radius-server local
     server enable
     nas 10.38.22.33 key +gqfn7cOnp83ulC8akPX/9XuVpItdisbnaoeOBiSfYs= encrypted
     user ckrad encrypted password uV3Hzwc1fkxN/uN2+d+U2VDuO00NGvoIguLbH8XYLt4=
    ntp authentication-key 10 md5 ckntpkey1
  And in `show log`, one `local6.warning` line for each rejection, carrying the line number
  in the startup configuration and the reason:
    startup-config: 7: username ckp7 privilege 7 password 8 $5$... -- Privilege level 15
    startup-config: 22: snmp-server community cktest  -- Community is not allowed in crypto secure-mode
    startup-config: 23: snmp-server user ckv3 ckgrp encrypted auth sha 0x05de... -- SHA-1 authentication is not allowed in crypto secure-mode
  `service telnet` is absent from that list, because phase 1 had already rewritten it to
  `no service telnet`.

  Phase 3, disabling. `no crypto secure-mode` gives the same advisory
  (`% Save the config and restart the system for this change to take effect.`),
  `show secure-mode` flips to `disabled` at once, and after `write` and `reload` the line
  is gone from both configurations.
  => STEP N3 done: the refused lines do NOT come back when secure mode is turned off.
     They were dropped from the saved configuration at phase 2. So a `write` while secure
     mode is on makes the loss permanent. This is the most important operational
     consequence in the case. Save a copy of the configuration before enabling secure mode.

     FINDING F-1: `show secure-mode` reads "enabled" before secure mode is in force. The
     device says in one breath that the change needs a save and a restart, and in the next
     that secure mode is enabled. At 09:50:27 `show secure-mode` read enabled while the
     SNMP GET still returned a value and the RADIUS request still got Access-Accept. The
     `show` command describes the configuration, not what is enforced. Anyone grading this
     feature from `show secure-mode` alone would believe it was all active when most of it
     was not.

