  | `snmp-server community cktest ro`            | accepted                                      | `% Community is not allowed in crypto secure-mode` |
  | the configured community                     | present in running-config                     | removed at the restart, logged by line number    |
  | `snmp-server user ... auth md5 ... priv des` | accepted                                      | `% MD5 authentication is not allowed in crypto secure-mode` |
  | `snmp-server user ... auth sha ... priv aes` | accepted                                      | `% SHA-1 authentication is not allowed in crypto secure-mode` |
  | `snmp-server user ... auth sha-256 ... priv aes` | not applicable                            | accepted, stored encrypted in running-config     |

  => STEP N5 PASS on the title's claim. SNMPv1 and v2c are really disabled. An SNMP GET
     that returned the software version before secure mode timed out after it, and the
     community was deleted from the configuration.

     FINDING F-3: secure mode is STRICTER than its own documentation. The documentation
     says only that MD5 and DES are not allowed for authentication. On this build SHA-1 is
     also refused, by name, and only SHA-256 is accepted. That is why the SNMPv3 user built
     in STEP 0 with `auth sha` was thrown out at the restart, although it used neither MD5
     nor DES. An operator who follows the documentation would expect that user to survive
     a restart. It does not.

     NOT RUN, and the reason: "an SNMPv3 request still succeeds under secure mode"
     could not be driven from this bench. Secure mode accepts only SHA-256 authentication,
     and the testbox's SNMP client software cannot use SHA-256. Both SNMP authentication
     protocols the box can speak are the two that secure mode refuses. Fixing that needs
     software installed on the box, which is the Test Engineer's action. This is a limit of
     the instrument, not a property of the DUT. The DUT ACCEPTED the SHA-256 user at the
     CLI and stored it, and that is the strongest statement this bench supports. It is a
     positive one.

     NOT RUN: DES on its own as a privacy protocol. The test line
     `auth md5 ... priv des ...` was rejected on its authentication keyword, so the parser
     never read the `priv des` part. DES alone was not retested with an accepted
     authentication protocol.

