STEP N11 -- the PKI store moves, and whether the move can be undone                   PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  In secure mode, read at 10:00:
    Trustpoint "default-selfsigned"   RSA root  FAF19010 A6B8B53B 7F6D7CAC F7222476 C26B2633
                                      ECDSA root 169FD0D1 DEA6E435 187FD4F6 31D65681 7B916D67
    Trustpoint "default-system"       (present)
    Trustpoint "local"                ABSENT
    hostkeys: ecdsa 384 SHA256:l7MKU3gCfcN0IechQWU8zwxfkZ5ySwsVAAgMiyQwlls   (ONE key)

  After `no crypto secure-mode` and a reload, read at 10:07 against the baseline taken at
  09:37 before anything was changed:
    Trustpoint "default-selfsigned"   RSA root  084B6549 62DB51AD 493DD993 4353370C FC88C5DD   IDENTICAL
                                      ECDSA root 59915621 05FBC4FB D1D63A5E 3AAE2EE2 2D40E6C6  IDENTICAL
    Trustpoint "default-system"       present                                                  IDENTICAL
    Trustpoint "local"                BACK: RSA  C29CD492 253DF5BD F849819E F12866D8 792C90BE  IDENTICAL
                                            ECDSA 906D4096 FB243C13 F727C758 7F41DF0D CBCF1FB5 IDENTICAL
                                      server keys server-default / server-default-ecdsa        IDENTICAL
    hostkeys: rsa     3072 SHA256:iWG4XK1CWjkpR9bQM5MBAuYtiyt/PyBvY3yollNf54E                  IDENTICAL
              ecdsa    384 SHA256:oer/uwNjbwLIMUamUN5UW92BuJA81Cyh6ScF/x6RSrc                  IDENTICAL
              ed25519  256 SHA256:VhI6K5ajJHpjKXJ/vCsQSZPvEnlxxrOeMkrZRDXMgq4                  IDENTICAL

  => STEP N11 PASS: the move is reversible. A PKI listing taken INSIDE secure mode
     describes the secure-mode store, not the flash store. It looks exactly like
     catastrophic key loss: trustpoint `local` gone, two of the three host keys gone, and
     `default-selfsigned` apparently regenerated. It is not key loss. The flash store is
     set aside intact and a separate secure-mode store with a single ECDSA host key is used
     instead. Leaving secure mode restores the original store byte for byte. Do not grade
     the flash store from a read taken inside secure mode.

     FINDING F-7, and it is good news. One thing did not come back, and that is useful. The
     `cktls` enrolment under trustpoint `local` had no CLI command that could remove it on
     this build. After the round trip it is gone:
       before : Local RADIUS server user enrollments ready for export
                   cktls
       after  : the block is absent, and the trustpoint and both certificate chains are back
     So a secure-mode round trip clears that enrolment without touching the trustpoint. The
     thing no CLI could delete was deleted by turning secure mode on and off again.

     One piece of residue, recorded: `dir nvs:` after the round trip shows a 350-byte
     `hostkey` file dated Oct 27 2026 20:53:11. That is the secure-mode ECDSA host key,
     created at the secure-mode boot and not removed when secure mode was turned off. It is
     key material the device generated itself, so it was deliberately left in place. It is
     inert while secure mode is off.

STEP N12 -- the local RADIUS server is withdrawn                                      PASS / NOT RUN (802.1X half)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  | leg                                 | normal mode                                   | secure mode                                      |
  | ----------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------ |
  | Access-Request from the box         | `<- Access-Accept id=21 in 0.001s             | `<- NO REPLY after 5.0s (x1) [ICMP port          |
  |                                     | authenticator=VALID (responder holds the      | unreachable: nothing listening on that UDP       |
  |                                     | secret)`, exit 0                              | port]`, exit 1                                   |
  | `show radius local-server user`     | `Total users: 1 ... ckrad`                    | returns nothing at all                           |
  | `radius-server local` in config mode| accepted                                      | `% radius-server local is not supported in       |
  |                                     |                                               | secure mode.`                                    |
  | the configured block                | 5 lines in running-config                     | removed at the restart, announced in the boot    |
  |                                     |                                               | warning list                                     |

  => STEP N12 PASS: the local RADIUS server stops answering under secure mode. The ICMP
     port-unreachable reply is the strongest available proof that nothing is listening. It
     is stronger than silence, because it shows the host was reachable and the UDP port was
     closed.

     FINDING F-8: a refused command still changes the CLI mode. `radius-server local`
     printed its refusal and the prompt became `awplus(config-radsrv)#` anyway:
awplus(config)#radius-server local
  % radius-server local is not supported in secure mode.
  ...
awplus(config-radsrv)#end
     Nothing was created. The running configuration afterwards has no `radius-server` line
     at all, so the effect is cosmetic. But a script that decides its next command from the
     prompt would be misled.

     NOT RUN: the 802.1X half of this limb, meaning what the withdrawal does to a
     supplicant on port1.0.48. There was no supplicant configuration on that port when this
     run started, so there was nothing to break, and building one was outside this case's
     scope. The RADIUS measurement on the wire is the substitute, and for this limb it is
     the stronger instrument: it shows the server stopped answering, which is the mechanism
     that an 802.1X failure would only be a symptom of. The consequence at the supplicant
     is named here so that it is not read as covered.

