FINDINGS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  F-1  `show secure-mode` reads "enabled" before secure mode is in force. Between the
       enable and the restart the device reports the feature on while only the telnet
       server is actually stopped. SNMPv2c, the local RADIUS server and the whole CLI
       parser are untouched.                                              STEP N1, STEP N3
  F-2  The telnet CLIENT still runs in secure mode. The documentation says change request
       CR-84010 disables it. It opened a socket and received a reset from the far end.
                                                                                  STEP N4
  F-3  Secure mode is stricter than its own documentation: SHA-1 is refused for SNMPv3, not
       just MD5 and DES. Only SHA-256 is accepted. An SNMPv3 user built per the
       documentation is deleted at the next restart.                              STEP N5
  F-4  The "rsa1 and dsa not supported" rule cannot be confirmed here, because they are
       absent in NORMAL mode too.                                        STEP 0, STEP N7
  F-5  The CLI help surface reflects no secure-mode restriction at all. Every probed prefix
       returns byte-identical completions in both modes, and the parser then rejects what
       the help offered.                                     STEP 0, STEP N7, N8, N10
  F-6  Neither documented route to the cryptographic self-test exists.
       `crypto secure-mode-selftest` is `% Unrecognized command` in secure mode as well as
       normal mode, and `show post` adds a `SECURE-MODE POST` heading with nothing under
       it.                                                                       STEP N10
  F-7  A secure-mode round trip deletes a PKI enrolment that no CLI can remove, while the
       trustpoint and both certificate chains return intact. This one is benign and
       useful.                                                                   STEP N11
  F-8  A refused command still changes the CLI mode. `radius-server local` prints its
       refusal and the prompt becomes `awplus(config-radsrv)#` anyway.           STEP N12

